=== 1. A program is a list, and it runs ===
  as data:  ['+', 1, ['*', 2, 3]]
  as text:  (+ 1 (* 2 3))
  evaluated: 7

=== 2. Quote turns a program back into data ===
  (quote (+ 1 (* 2 3)))    -> ['+', 1, ['*', 2, 3]]
  The SAME list. Evaluated it is 7; quoted it is a three-element list.

=== 3. Programs are inspectable, because they are just lists ===
  (+ 1 (* 2 3)) has 7 nodes
  its operator is '+', its second argument is ['*', 2, 3]

=== 4. A MACRO: a function from program to program ===
  you wrote:   (unless (> x 100) (* x 2))
  macro made:  (if (> x 100) 0 (* x 2))
  which runs to: 14
  'unless' is not in the evaluator. A user added it, in six lines,
  without touching the language implementation.

=== 5. Why Python cannot do this as easily ===
  In Python, 'if x > 100: ...' is syntax, parsed into an AST object
  by the interpreter before your code ever sees it. In Scheme the
  program was ALREADY the data structure, so no parsing step stands
  between you and it. That is homoiconicity, and macros are the payoff.
